KW  - Machine learning
KW  - Databases
KW  - Query optimization
KW  - Learned index structures
KW  - Self-tuning database systems
KW  - Adaptive systems
AB  - Integrating machine learning (ML) techniques into core components of database management systems (DBMS) is reshaping the architectural foundations of modern data platforms. This survey provides a system-level analysis of how ML methods are embedded in indexing structures, query optimizers, self-tuning mechanisms, and anomaly detection modules. Using a structured methodology covering peer-reviewed research published between January 2020 and November 2025, we screened 214 studies and included 162 peer-reviewed research papers complemented by two technical resources. We identified dominant integration patterns and examined representative implementations, including learned indexes, ML-based cost models, adaptive tuning strategies, and workload forecasting mechanisms. A functional taxonomy categorizes the interaction types between ML components and DBMS subsystems and captures their architectural roles and coupling strategies. Evaluation practices, including system-level benchmarks, workload generators, and performance metrics, were reviewed to assess their comparability and reproducibility across studies. Comparative tables and case studies synthesize technical insights across deployment scenarios, and open research challenges related to lifecycle management, inference constraints, and workload variability are also discussed. A final discussion contrasts this survey with prior work and outlines implications for future research and engineering practice.
