KW  - Coastal saline soil
KW  - Feature selection
KW  - Multivariate models
KW  - Sustainable agriculture
KW  - Spectroscopy
KW  - Soil properties
AB  - Soil pH and Electrical Conductivity (EC) are critical indicators of soil health, affecting nutrient availability, crop productivity, and overall ecosystem functioning. Accurate and scalable estimation of these properties across varying soil-to-water ratios is vital for precision agriculture and environmental monitoring. This study proposes ExGAPS (Explainable Greedy Adaptive SHAP-Guided PLSR), a unified and optimized Explainable Artificial Intelligence (EAI) modeling framework that integrates Partial Least Squares Regression (PLSR) with SHapley Additive exPlanations (SHAP) for the prediction of soil pH and EC using hyperspectral reflectance data. Unlike conventional feature selection approaches, ExGAPS designs a dynamic, SHAP-guided wrapper algorithm that iteratively eliminates less informative spectral bands based on model feedback. This framework performs adaptive optimization of PLSR components and the feature subset. It enables simultaneous, high-accuracy estimation of multiple soil parameters (pH and EC) across various dilution ratios — saturation extract, 1:1, 1:2, 1:2.5, and 1:5 — while maintaining model interpretability. Ablation experiments with PLSR, Support Vector Regression (SVR), and their hybrid versions (PLSR with SVR) — with and without SHAP — demonstrate that the SHAP-augmented PLSR (ExGAPS) consistently yields superior performance, with R2 values ranging from 0.625 to 0.793 for pH and 0.750 to 0.874 for EC. By incorporating Explainable AI using SHAP, this framework ensures interpretability, adaptiveness, and generalizability, providing a robust and scalable solution for real-time soil quality assessment using hyperspectral sensing in precision agriculture.
