KW  - Intracranial pressure
KW  - Electrocardiogram
KW  - Arterial blood pressure
KW  - Deep learning
KW  - Noninvasive reconstruction
AB  - Continuous monitoring of intracranial pressure (ICP) is an important objective in the management of neurological disorders such as traumatic brain injury, subarachnoid hemorrhage, and subdural hematoma; however, reliable noninvasive reconstruction of detailed ICP waveforms remains challenging. In this study, we present an end-to-end deep learning framework for ICP waveform estimation that fuses electrocardiogram (ECG) and arterial blood pressure (ABP) signals under a controlled offline experimental setting. Publicly available multiparameter physiological recordings containing synchronized ECG, ABP, and ICP signals were used. After standardized preprocessing, fixed-length sliding-window segmentation, normalization, and ECG–ABP peak-based phase alignment, signal segments were fed into an improved Wave-U-Net model for supervised ICP waveform regression. Model training employed a dynamically weighted composite loss designed to stabilize waveform reconstruction and preserve both temporal and morphological consistency. Under a subject-wise validation protocol, the proposed framework achieved high segment-level agreement between estimated and reference ICP waveforms on the held-out subject, as reflected by a Pearson correlation coefficient of 0.932 and low reconstruction error under the offline evaluation protocol. Comparative analyses further indicate that incorporating ECG alongside ABP improves phase alignment and fine-grained waveform reconstruction relative to a single-channel ABP model. Given the limited cohort size and the use of highly overlapping sliding windows, the reported results should not be interpreted as evidence of population-level generalization or direct clinical applicability. Instead, this work serves as a methodological feasibility study, demonstrating the potential of multimodal physiological signal fusion for ICP waveform estimation and motivating future investigations using larger and more diverse datasets.
