KW  - Integrity learning
KW  - Remote sensing
AB  - Multi-source remote sensing data fusion shows great potential in object localization, identification and classification tasks. However, data constraints such as a lack of redundancy and discriminative information have hindered the development and application of its integrity. In addition, the simultaneous processing of high-frequency structural information and low-frequency contour information has received limited attention. We define the concept of integrity by introducing micro and macro levels. Specifically, at the micro level, the model should highlight all the parts belonging to an object. Meanwhile, at the macro level, the model needs to discover all objects in an image. To facilitate integrity learning for multi-source data, we design an Integrated Learning for Multi-scale Awareness fusion network (ILMA), which explores three important components for learning strong integrity features. (1) The Multi-scale Feature Fusion (MFF) captures multi-scale features and mines the basis of the whole object. (2) The Macro Integrity Learning (MaIL) captures the low-frequency information to build the ability of remote association. Meanwhile, the Micro Integrity Learning (MiIL) captures high-frequency information. (3) The Macro–Micro Verification (MMV) deeply complements the high-frequency and low-frequency information interactions to further determine whether the object features have strong consistency or not. Extensive experimental results demonstrate that ILMA is effectively compatible with feature representations of different multi-source data types on the three benchmark datasets and outperforms existing state-of-the-art methods in classification.
ER  - 

