AB  - Efficient and accurate identification of walking sheep entering and exiting pens daily helps herders obtain appearance information on lost sheep, enabling effective search and avoiding potential arguments in grassland grazing. Reported studies have focused on counting, but lack efficient and accurate identification methods for walking sheep. In this study, a set of methods for walking sheep detection and tracking, back image sequence extraction, filtering, and identity recognition is proposed, where the flock is shaped into ordered multiple single-column sheep streams by a diversion channel. Based on this, the MEB-YOLOv8n detection model (incorporating MobileViT, ECA (efficient channel attention), and BiFormer into YOLOv8n (You Only Look Once version 8)), a back image extraction and filtering method, and the CM-MViT identification model (incorporating ECA and EMA (efficient multi-scale attention) into MobileViT) were proposed. The performance of five sheep back image sequence extraction methods from video was compared. The results show that the MEB-YOLOv8n model improves the mAP@0.5 (mean Average Precision) by 0.48% and reduces the parameters by 60% compared to original YOLOv8n; extracting back images from detection box is more accurate than tracking box, and back images filtering with Laplace variance over 35 improves accuracy by 1.32%; the CM-MViT model improves the accuracy by 1.31% over original MobileViT. The entire method reaches 99.05% accuracy when the sheep's back images are extracted within the “1/6-5/6” screen range. Compared with reported methods, the above method has the advantages of anti-occlusion, high efficiency, and accuracy, providing important support for establishing a multi-objective walking sheep identification system.
