AB  - Speech intelligibility can be measured by the Speech Transmission Index (STI), a complex and expensive type of assessment. In this sense, STI predictive models that employ Reverberation Time (RT) do not adequately consider the spectral effects of the Room Impulse Response (RIR) and background noise (BGN). This study aims to predict STI values applying a deep one-dimensional convolutional neural network (1D CNN). To address the challenge of performing in situ STI measurements, a Deep Transfer Learning (DTL) algorithm, termed Non-parametric Projective-Adaptive Variational Minimization (NP-PAVM), is proposed. This algorithm optimizes a generative model trained on simulated data and adapts it to real-world measured data using Variational Autoencoders (VAEs). These, in turn, generate an aggregated posterior distribution of STI in the source domain, which is used to parameterize the target domain, while a Kernel Principal Component Regression (KPCR) establishes a mapping between the source and target domains. The NP-PAVM was validated using 84 RT measurements and 277 STI measurements from 13 classrooms, and its performance was assessed on a test set comprising 720 simulated classrooms. The 1D CNN assessment employing NP-PAVM-based DTL demonstrated significant accuracy improvements, in which the 1D CNN achieved optimal prediction metrics during training, with a Coefficient of Determination (R2) of 0.975, a Mean Squared Error (MSE) of 0.002, and a Mean Absolute Error (MAE) of 0.033. For a 10-fold cross-validation, the R2 and MSE metrics were 0.917 ± 0.023 and 0.007 ± 0.002, respectively. The proposed NP-PAVM demonstrates significant potential as a practical tool for classroom STI assessments, leveraging standard RT measurement instrumentation to acquire richer acoustic data.
