AB  - Infrared small target detection (IRSTD) remains a highly challenging task with broad applications across numerous domains. Recent significant advancements in deep learning have greatly propelled progress in this field. However, existing approaches have yet to adequately address the effective utilization of edge information within global modeling frameworks, notably lacking a mechanism to explicitly integrate multi-scale edge priors into a selective state space model(Mamba)-based global modeling. To address this limitation, we propose Mamba-IRSTD, a novel encoder–decoder architecture specifically designed to enhance edge clarity in IRSTD tasks. In the encoder stage, we introduce a dual-residual-path Mamba block that effectively combines the complementary strengths of convolutional neural networks and Mamba-based state space models for efficient global context modeling. This hybrid design enables the integration of both local and global information, thereby enriching the extracted feature representations. Furthermore, a multi-scale feature adaptive aggregation block is incorporated to strengthen the representation of features across multiple scales. In the decoder stage, we design an edge-aware attention module coupled with a multi-scale shape-adaptive fusion head, which enables the model to focus explicitly on edge features, thus improving target localization and enabling more precise target reconstruction. Extensive experiments on the three public IRSTD datasets validate the superior accuracy and computational efficiency of our method. The source code will be made publicly available at: https://github.com/Fidods/Mamba-IRSTD.
