AB  - Epileptic seizures seriously threaten patient safety, making reliable seizure detection and early warning essential for clinical monitoring. However, existing electroencephalogram (EEG)-based methods often suffer from noise interference, limited feature adaptability, and strong dependence on labeled data. To address these challenges, this study proposes a dual-model intelligent diagnosis framework. As the first component, a Butterworth-Residual Network-Gated Recurrent Unit and Gated Adaptive Fusion (BRGGa) network is developed for automated seizure detection. BRGGa integrates Butterworth bandpass filtering to suppress noise in raw EEG with a hybrid Residual Network (ResNet) and Gated Recurrent Unit (GRU) architecture to capture spatial morphology variations and long-range temporal dependencies. Further building upon these representations, a gating-based adaptive fusion mechanism dynamically reweights heterogeneous features, enabling robust and personalized ensemble learning. Experiments on the Children Hospital Boston-Massachusetts Institute of Technology (CHB-MIT) and Bonn datasets show that BRGGa achieves average detection accuracies of 99.37% and 98.17%, respectively, demonstrating consistent performance across different evaluation settings. Unlike training-dependent early warning, a Dynamic Channel Network Marker (DCNM) is proposed for seizure early warning. By leveraging the critical slowing down theory, DCNM identifies sub-channels exhibiting pronounced fluctuation and correlation enhancement prior to seizure onset, enabling training-free and patient-specific early warning. On the CHB-MIT and Siena datasets, DCNM achieves sensitivities of 96.02% and 97.73%, respectively, with an average warning time of 20 min, outperforming recent seizure early warning methods. These results demonstrate that the proposed dual-model framework provides an effective and reliable solution for accurate seizure detection and personalized early warning in real-world clinical monitoring systems.
