AB  - This paper proposes a fixed-time reinforcement learning fault-tolerant control strategy for a class of uncertain nonlinear systems with actuator saturation and time-varying bias faults. A control framework based on actor–critic neural networks is developed, in which the critic network approximates a discounted performance index that penalizes tracking errors and control energy, and the actor network generates a nominal control input in real time. To enhance robustness with respect to actuator faults, an adaptive fault compensation mechanism is incorporated to mitigate the effects of input saturation and time-varying bias. By combining a synthesized auxiliary error with a state-dependent nonlinear gain and power-type feedback terms, the fixed-time stability of the closed-loop system is rigorously established, and an explicit upper bound on the convergence time, independent of the initial state, is derived. The proposed control strategy is validated on a hardware-in-the-loop experimental platform through two case studies, namely quadrotor position tracking and launch vehicle attitude control. In the quadrotor example, the proposed method reduces the settling time by approximately 48.7% compared with a conventional proportional–integral–derivative controller and by 33.7% compared with a reinforcement-learning-based fault-tolerant control method without fixed-time design. In the launch vehicle example, the settling time is decreased by about 24.1% relative to a sliding mode control scheme and by 50% relative to the reinforcement-learning-based fault-tolerant baseline. In both cases, the proposed strategy achieves significantly faster convergence while ensuring smooth and bounded control inputs, demonstrating its effectiveness and advantages in terms of convergence speed, robustness, and fault tolerance.
