KW  - Physics-Informed Machine Learning
KW  - Gaussian Process
KW  - Conjugate gradient optimization
KW  - Acceleration of vector sequence
KW  - Operational parametric equations
AB  - Physics-Informed Machine Learning (PIML) combines the predictive power of data-driven models with the foundational principles of physics to develop accurate and efficient models for complex dynamical systems. A prominent instance of this approach, Physics-Informed Gaussian Processes (PIGP), operates within a Bayesian framework to solve parametric operator equations, providing posterior probabilistic predictions and uncertainty quantification. However, a major computational bottleneck arises from the optimization of the non-convex likelihood function, which requires repeated O(n3) operations to compute the inverse and determinant of the covariance matrix. Although gradient-based methods such as the conjugate gradient algorithm have been employed to address this challenge, conventional implementations often lack efficiency and fail to achieve optimal convergence. This study addresses this challenge by enhancing classical conjugate gradient methods for PIGP hyperparameter optimization. The main goal is to accelerate convergence and reduce computational cost without compromising predictive accuracy. Key contributions include: (i) step-length normalization to stabilize ill-conditioned likelihood landscapes; (ii) four vector-based acceleration techniques inspired by Aitken’s Δ2 process; (iii) a theorem establishing necessary and sufficient conditions for convergence under standard descent and line-search assumptions; and (iv) validation on parametric operator equations, including the heat equation and Drosophila gene diffusion, showing up to 95% fewer iterations and 83% fewer function evaluations compared to the baseline “Minimize” optimizer, while maintaining or improving accuracy. The novelty of this work lies in the combination of step-length normalization and vector-based acceleration schemes, which are theoretically guaranteed to converge within the PIGP framework. Empirical validation on the heat equation and Drosophila gene reaction–diffusion model demonstrates dramatically reduced computational cost while maintaining or improving accuracy compared to classic optimizers, such as the “Minimize” algorithm. These innovations enhance the practical applicability of PIGP in scientific and engineering domains, where both reliability and efficiency are critical.
ER  - 

